# The sdist ships the test suite, and tests/test_metadata_consistency.py reads
# the citation metadata from the project root. Without these files the suite
# fails when run from an unpacked sdist, which is exactly what a reviewer or a
# packager does to check the release. Everything the suite reads is therefore
# part of the distribution.
include LICENSE
include README.md
include CHANGELOG.md
include KNOWN_ISSUES.md
include CONTRIBUTING.md
include CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
include CITATION.cff
include codemeta.json
include .zenodo.json
include reproducible_harvesting.md
include AUDIT_REPORT.md

# The offline-enforcement plugin is invoked as `pytest -p tools.no_network`, so
# it has to travel with the tests it guards.
recursive-include tools *.py

# tests/test_docs_examples.py executes every example in the guide, so the
# guide has to travel with the tests.
recursive-include docs *.md

recursive-include tests *.py

# tests/test_notebook.py reads notebooks/corpusslr_colab.ipynb as an artifact
# (valid JSON, every code cell compiles, no saved credential). Leaving the
# notebook out of the sdist turned those 18 tests into collection ERRORS when
# the suite was run from an unpacked archive: the reviewer's own check.
recursive-include notebooks *.ipynb

# paper/ holds the article, its bibliography, its runnable listing and the
# checks that verify it. tools/build_reviewer_package.py names those files in
# its inventory, and tests/test_reviewer_package.py fails when an inventory
# entry is absent - correctly, since a silently skipped file would still be
# listed in the package README and the omission would surface only for the
# reviewer. Build products (.aux, .log, .pdf) are excluded: the PDF is
# regenerated by the documented command.
recursive-include paper *.tex *.bib *.py *.md
recursive-include paper/figures *.png

# examples/ ships the .py walkthroughs AND review_config.json, which is the
# configuration `corpusslr run` is documented against and which
# test_the_example_config_shipped_with_the_package_is_valid() checks. A
# *.py-only rule left the .json behind, so that test silently skipped from an
# unpacked sdist: the one place the check matters was the one place it
# did not run.
recursive-include examples *.py *.json *.yaml *.yml

# Validation inputs and recomputed metrics: the provenance of every accuracy
# figure quoted in the documentation. The large raw corpus dump is excluded --
# it is regenerable by validation/eval_multidomain.py and would dominate the
# archive.
recursive-include validation *.py *.csv *.md
exclude validation/multidomain_raw.jsonl.gz

# The three fifteen-discipline archives are the reason that evaluation
# reproduces without network access: validation/eval_domains_15.py reads them
# and regenerates domains_15_final.csv, the table behind the headline accuracy
# figures. 6.7 MB is the price of a reviewer being able to check those numbers
# instead of taking them on trust. The older multidomain dump above stays
# excluded: it is superseded and stands behind no published figure.
include validation/domains_social_raw.jsonl.gz
include validation/domains_stem_raw.jsonl.gz
include validation/domains_nature_hum_raw.jsonl.gz

include SUBMISSION_CHECKLIST.md
include SUPPLEMENTARY.md

# PUBLISHING.md is the release procedure: how this tree is put on GitHub,
# which workflows run, how the tag and the Zenodo DOI are minted, and which
# numbers each step is checked against. A packager or a reviewer holding only
# the sdist otherwise has no way to see how the published artefact was made.
include PUBLISHING.md

# The documentation-site generator travels with the tests that guard it.
# tests/test_site.py imports tools/build_site.py and builds the real site from
# the source documents, so leaving either behind turns 109 tests into
# collection errors when the suite is run from an unpacked sdist. The
# `recursive-include tools *.py` rule above already ships the generator; this
# note records why it must keep doing so.
#
# The generator also reads the two documents below, which no earlier rule
# covers: MANUSCRIPT.md is not rendered as a page but is quoted by the number
# audit, and validation/multidomain_validation.md is rendered as a page and is
# picked up by the `recursive-include validation *.md` rule above.
include MANUSCRIPT.md

# The generated site itself is never distributed. It is rebuilt from the
# markdown by `python tools/build_site.py` in under a second, and shipping a
# copy would let the published HTML drift from the documents it renders.
prune site
# The manuscript figures are the provenance of every accuracy claim.
recursive-include validation *.png
recursive-include validation *.json

prune build_check

# The reviewer package is a product built FROM the repository by
# tools/build_reviewer_package.py, not a part of the release: it duplicates the
# manuscript, the validation tables, the figures and the package source into one
# 13 MB tree, and shipping it inside the sdist would roughly double the archive
# with a second copy of files already in it. It rebuilds byte-identically at any
# time, so nothing is lost by leaving it out. The repository path should add
# both lines to .gitignore for the same reason.
prune reviewer_package
exclude corpusslr_reviewer_package.zip

global-exclude *.py[cod] __pycache__/* .DS_Store
